lic paraboloid top which I think  did  described it approvingly as having zing  [Laughter]  which really made me laugh  um  I mean it got attention but  I didn't find any any mention at all of  the fact that this was a especially  secure  well-designed you know in intended to  you know deal with nuclear shock waves  I I and that's a good question because I  don't understand why nobody would have  played that up  and then my more general question is  probably for all of you one of the  things that I think is so interesting  about the libraries that it had very  different institutional homes being in  the military and then now being part of  the NIH and I'm wondering if in your  research you came across any images or  documents or accounts that speak to the  ways in which the institutional home can  shape the culture and if there was  anything about kind of disentangling  from the army that came through as  either particularly fraught or  complicated that kind of thing  the gentleman I mentioned earlier Brad  Rogers who was the last army officer and  who  um  sort of oversaw the the transfer  there's an oral history which was  actually done by the medical Library  Association there's also an oral history  of Estelle brodman who was basically  head of reference at the time and Rogers  brought her from from the University to  work here are very deeply involved in  this and there are also sources from  outside  the library places like the New York  Academy of Medicine and the other major  medical libraries of the time saying hey  if you're going to be a National Library  of Medicine here's what we expect you to  do and I think the most famous fruits of  that particular moment  are the establishment of the Regional  Medical Library system and one of the  you see in that lovely video one of the  original maps of the rml districts that  we would act not as a leader in the  military sense but a leader in the  intellectual sense of giving a point of  focus and also an infra Library loan  system that was unique then is unique  now to get things to people without the  normal restrictions of you need to know  exactly where it is and the dockline  system still exists and allows the  fastest response possible for people who  need an infant Library loan request for  a patient who may be literally bleeding  on the table and you see that in  correspondence in and out  and in the oral histories we have which  are partially our work but also done by  the medical Library association's oral  history committee and their papers are  in fact stored at nlm  no please  so you mentioned the the military  culture uh and whether there was a  difference one thing that that came out  uh in the research of this book and it  was actually I think in Anne rothfeld's  chapter was that  when this was the military you know the  Army Medical Library was staffed by Army  personnel and so in in times of War army  Personnel often have better things or  other things to do instead of working at  a library so they got pulled away the  Army you know the active duty soldiers  were called off to World War one and  again to World War II but um when they  were called off to World War One the  impact of that was that they were  backfilled by women primarily and so  this was the first time in the history  of the library that the staff was  predominantly filled with women who  worked as Librarians and technicians and  did everything in the library and so  that that was kind of a turning point  and and uh some of the the women who  came in both you know as uh  Physicians who brought their medical  expertise to you know work in the the  indexing and the cataloging  um  and women who had expertise as  Librarians really  kind of turned or started the Turning of  the library from this Army institution  into a real professional library and it  made a huge difference and you know that  so that that was a point when I think  the the military culture uh started to  give way to a more professional civilian  culture and and really the library  started to focus on what it could be and  and started to grow into the institution  that it eventually became  okay thank you okay two points related  to the moving of the library to  Cleveland at the National Gallery of  author pictures of the loading of the  paintings of the National Gallery onto  local moving vans to be brought to the  Bookworm mansion in in North Carolina I  was wondering whether other institutions  like the Library of Congress were also  moved and the converse of that is was  the building the red old red brick  building used for other War related  activities when the library was not  there during the war  and in fact as the original NIH building  sound at Navy Hill were used during the  war  the red brick wasn't emptied the staff  continued to work there it was only the  items that were seen as being culturally  important so it was Dr free up space  then  um well there was a certain amount of  that as well because the library was  overfilled in fact the library began  microfilming its collection very early  on in the 1930s not to provide a backup  but to provide space but Dr Jones and  his staff and core functions like the  index Medicus the the indexing of  medical literature remained and  um  in DC when  when the books came back those books  that had been in Cleveland became the  core of what is now the history of  medicine division so only the older  books went out and in fact one person  from  DC staff went to Cleveland the rest were  people were hired on the spot I  mentioned Arthur schulian because she's  the most famous but everybody else was  hired out there including John eschman  the um the book binder  so the red brick was not emptied they  simply took that stuff and I'm sure that  there were other offices in there all  sorts of things were in and out of the  red brick wasn't a certain General's  office Army surge General's office in  the red brick for a while I kind of I  kind of think it was  um and so was the pension office right  that's right so um you know it's wartime  in the empty office isn't going to be  empty for very long  so we're nearly to the end of our  program but there's one more question  that we received that I'd appreciated if  we could address because it's a really  important one during the preparation of  our book what did we learn what did we  learn anything that differed from our  earlier histories  I I have a if I can answer that quickly  so one thing that I felt was very  different than the seminal history of  the library which is Windham miles  history going up to 1982 which was  published in 1982. uh that's very much  an Institutional history now our book is  too but uh miles it differs from Miles  book in two ways first it's ours is  Illustrated and miles is only mildly  Illustrated but it's also miles is far  too often written in the passive voice  and personally as a historian I always  try to write as we all do all good  writers do try to write in the active  voice and give agency to the narrative  give agency to the actions that are that  are being written about and I felt  really strongly that this book not only  needed to be engaging and for the  general audience but give agency to the  generations of individuals who worked at  the library rather than say the library  did X Y and Z it certainly did but more  than that it was the leaders of the  library who who led the staff and the  staff the expertise of the staff who  drove the library to achieve certain  things over the course of its history  and I felt that this book differed  fundamentally because it gave agency to  the staff it gave them a voice and it  gave them it put faces on on our  predecessors and and also dedicating the  book to the staff today the staff passed  and the staff in the future was a  gesture toward the agency given to these  public servants of which we all are  um in the work that that has been done  what's done today and what we've done in  the future so so I addressed that  Ken please  so I I would add to that that the the  the method that we took in in  researching this book uh kind of  building on the image collection and and  finding images that could tell the story  uh meant that we necessarily had to tell  a different story  um and so I think what we what we  uncovered in our research for this book  is is quite different from  the more traditional scholarly history  that window miles did because  the the the directions that we went were  really kind of  laid out for us and guided by those  images and so some of the things that  that came out that you don't find really  highlighted in in Miles book are things  like the diversity of the step you know  when you're pulling up these images and  you're looking at at uh  the indexing staff in 1948 and  if you if you are just reading about the  indexing staff and it just you know even  if names are listed or something you  don't know what that what that group of  people looks like you don't know who's  who's involved there and when you see  the images of it and you see that hey  you know this is really a dynamic and  diverse and eclectic group of people  it it highlights where the strength of  the library came from in those times and  I think that those are things that are  are lost in just a purely text based  research so I think that's that's  something that really uh struck me as we  did this uh did the research and  particularly working with Jeff to go  through and edit and select those images  because there were so many that we  weren't able to choose but you know  every one of those images still told a  story and the the stories are are vastly  uh  hmm  not contradictory they're different but  they're complementary to what we had  read in in Miles book  I learned anything new because I'd spent  a lot of time going through the history  of the library anyway but I was reminded  of a few things  and some of them were the you know we  have a few very famous directors who  were here for a long time and you have  people like Jones who's not often  remembered as a great figure in the  library about who really did an awful  lot with very little and there's certain  poignancy to him being asked to stay on  past retirement  and there's another  thing that I I knew but I was reminded  you know we we all know who the longest  serving directors of the library were  but we tend to forget the man who had  the shortest tenure as director of the  Army Medical Library he was given the  job he was already ill with what turned  out to be peritonitis and died before he  actually ever sat in the office as  director of the library and his name was  Walter Reed  well we're at our time and I want to  thank everyone here in lipsit for coming  to our program this afternoon thank you  all for your interest in this book and  again to my colleagues here and everyone  who contributed to this publication  around our institution I want to thank  again Dr Patricia Brennan for her  introductory remarks and welcoming all  of us and supporting this project and to  all of you watching remotely thank you  very much and all of you who I hope will  watch this archive video cast in the  future thank you for your support your  interest and we hope you enjoy this book  and add it to many future summer reading  lists thank you  [Applause]

